nd up building production systems  that have inconsistent data between data  stores between data centers they will  not scale they will lose data repeatedly  right all of these things happen in a  lot of the production systems we build  simply because for whatever reason is an  industry we're still comfortable  building systems that flat-out violate  the laws of physics and that is very  very sad and more importantly there's  really  no reason for that next thing I feel we  have felt learned that because  distributed systems are by nature large  and disaggregate systems many many  moving parts that move around  independently we fail to realize that we  need to invest in observability and I  think that over the last couple years  this has finally been getting sort of  the line light that it deserves but we  are still very much deficient in the  tools and the practices that are  required to make sense and reason about  the sort of assist large-scale micro  service based systems that we've that  were building and that we've built now  tools are becoming better five years ago  I would not be able to write this slide  five years ago we didn't have these  tools nowadays you actually have a lot  of open-source aftermarket tools that  you can employ to gain observability in  your systems you have tracing tools you  have tools for the aggregation and  visualization and graphing of metrics  you have centralized log aggregators you  have the elk stack and you have all  these aftermarket products like New  Relic etc that let you gain  observability into your system in a  sensible way unfortunately that's not  enough having tools is a very very good  thing because we didn't have those a few  years back but we're still as an  industry very deficient in the  methodology around how to employ these  tools to actually gain insight into the  systems that we build in order to make  sense of distributed system that's big  that has a lot of moving parts that are  that are doing stuff independently we  need to have a much more a much stronger  approach and much stronger reasoning in  order to answer questions such as what  should I even log right if you if you  take almost any production system and  you look at the sort of logging  statements that are included in the code  base they are I would say somewhere  between a valiant attempt and utterly  useless like we're not logging things  that anyone including ourselves actually  care about we log because we've  told that we need to log not because  we're planning on these logs to be  useful to us in the future in order to  debug the system and that's a problem we  also have don't have a rigorous  methodology about what it is we should  count  what sort of metrics do we care about  what effects our system what are the  KPIs for any particular production  system that you need to monitor so that  you know when the system is in trouble  or that it's outgrowing its scaling  envelope for its existing architecture  or any of these things these are hard  questions there are no easy answers but  I feel that for the most part most of us  have been ignoring those questions for  years and that is that is a problem  fortunately it also seems that this that  there's sort of a movement in the other  direction where a lot of us have  realized that it's very very hard to  gain any sense out of what's going on in  our systems and that is why we're  starting to invest in tools  what should we monitor and most  importantly the bottom line is once we  have all that data we are still severely  deficient in the phonology about how to  make sense of it right we have all these  metrics all these logs all these  different things coming out of our  system we don't really have a industry a  rigorous methodology that tells us what  to do about it what kind of questions we  need to ask how to aggregate metrics I'm  sorry to have to say this but a lot of a  lot of engineers and a lot of companies  are still perfectly comfortable doing  stuff like looking at average response  times which is the single most useless  metric on earth and if you don't know  why that's a single useless metric then  you will have proved my point right as  an industry we have still not properly  properly learned to deal with  distributed systems from an  observability standpoint and with all  those sort of unfortunate lacks of  learning I think there are still some  things we can do today to start  improving the state of affairs in our  various  and although the title is explosive none  of the things I'm gonna be talking about  for the remaining four and a half  minutes of this talk should come as any  surprise to you but before we talk about  my opinion on what it is we should be  doing let's do a quick recap on what it  is we're trying to accomplish here  we aim to minimize synchronization and  maximize team independence that is what  we want what's hard for us is to build  systems that are safe that deal  correctly with data that do not violate  the laws of physics that don't lose data  or get incorrect data in through through  faulty designs we want scalability  scalability is still not an easy thing  to deal with for a lot of these systems  and we want observability these are the  three things we want and it turns out  that we already have some tools that let  us design our systems in a way where the  two goals are met and the three things  we're struggling with are somewhat  mitigated and a lot of those techniques  circle round events it turns out events  are your friends and events make  everything that much simpler if you  designed your system with events in mind  and I will of course delve into what  that actually could actually mean then  you end up with systems that are more  resilient more observable less wrong and  are actually simpler that are easier to  reason about easier to think about  easier to build and easier to deal with  an events come into play in three major  ways in sort of systems we end up  building one way we can use events is in  modeling interactions between our  various services we all know and love  our PC and some use cases make perfect  sense for our PC and very little sense  for other things but it turns out that a  lot of the flows that are built into  your systems you can actually model as  services emitting and consuming events  again this should not be news right  we've we've had talks about you know  event based systems in this conference  and in every other conference over the  last 10 years now the advantage of  modeling modeling the interactions  between your services as events being  emitted and consumed is that an enforcer  strong context boundaries if you only  emit events and only consume events that  is the surface area of your service by  definition you have to seriously  consider what the what the context  boundaries for your service are right  otherwise you just can't reason about  what sort of events you need to emit and  what sort of events you need to consume  it also lets you like assuming you have  a scalable event bus or message broker  or whatever it is you use it also allows  you to scale your services independently  if service a publishes events and those  events are useful to services B and C  but in completely separate ways more  importantly services B and C might have  completely different scaling  characteristics and requirements you can  do that you can build those services  independently consume the same events  and scale those services in very very  different ways and because you're  omitting events and consuming events it  makes it that much easier to actually  observe what's going on in the system  because you can consume those events as  part of your observability story and  finally it also tends a synchronous  flows also tend to be tend to increase  system reliability especially for  complex flows the other way in which you  can use events that I highly recommend  you look into is essentially event  sourcing instead of storing your  business entities as mutable rows in a  database you can store them as a  successive sequence of events that  change the state of your your particular  business entity canonical example is of  course a bank account your bank account  is not your my English sucks today I  forgot the word it's not the number you  see when you look at your ATM and ask  how much money do I have left in my bank  account it's not your balance right your  bank account is the succession of events  succession of deposits and withdrawals  out of your bank account and putting all  those together give  the balance and there are a lot of  benefits to storing your data in that  way observability is built in as is  auditing right if you only save what  happened as opposed to do results of  that thing happening then you have  observability built into your system and  at let's see scale read and write use  cases independently which is the impetus  for the CQRS pattern on which there was  a talk yesterday at this very conference  the apparent downside of this is that it  precludes full consistency right if you  if you store events then ostensibly you  can't get full consistency out of it you  can only get eventual consistency out of  your systems I submit that that is a  good thing because unlike the narrow  field of software engineering virtually  nothing in life is fully consistent no  business that has more than say one  branch is fully consistent no bank no  bank account no banking system is fully  consistent they all have to deal with  the laws of physics and the reality that  in order to provide service you have to  give up consistency in order to get  availability right the world we live in  is essentially eventually consistent so  giving up on this facade the full  consistency that you're used to from  your traditional database systems and  your traditional architectures only ends  up with systems that are actually easier  to reason about and work more reliably  and faster at that the last use case for  events is modeling workflows this is  what's called the saga pattern and is a  very big topic that's totally outside  the scope suffice to say that for the  most part sagas give you what you need  or what you use to want out of database  transactions at a fraction of the cost  and they're not super complex to build  again large topic we have no time to go  into it but I would highly recommend  digging into the saga pattern it is a  very very useful tool to have in your  toolbox  the bottom line is for my money you  should sweat the principles not the  implementation details for the last ten  years we've been mostly focusing on what  it entails to build a microcircuits  based system we've been talking about  RPC subsystems and monitoring in circuit  breakers and all of these things and all  of these things over the last few years  have been commoditized if you're  building a micro service framework for  your organization you're probably  wasting your time and wasting your  company's money a lot of these tools can  be can now be taken off the shelf and  integrated into your micro service  substrate and they will do a much better  job than you would have done five years  ago so instead of spending the time  learning about how to implement circuit  breakers and how to do HTTP 200 reliably  and efficiently and whether you should  see relize stuff as Jason or protobuf or  thrift just pick any one of those tools  and spend your time investing in studies  spend your time learning the laws of  physics namely the cap theorem and  various trade-offs that are involved  with it spend your time learning how to  model your domains how to do event  storming which is nice technique for for  starting to draw those boundaries around  your different contexts learn about  events or seeing in CQRS learn about  sagas if you spend your time doing these  things you'll be much better off you'll  end up building systems that are more  performant more reliable you will enable  your organization to grow faster you  will be able to build additional product  lines based on your pre-existing  products faster and more reliably you'll  be better off all around I will leave  you with these two lengths one of them  is totally a shameless plug by the way  but first there is this talk by Deven  CTO of gilt Michael price ik which is  called design micro service  architectures the right way he's dishing  out a lot of wisdom there and then if  you're interested in events or saying  there's my own talk called an abridged  guide to event sourcing which will  hopefully be useful to you in getting  started and I can flat-out guarantee if  any of these things sounds hard or  sounds scary to you they actually are  not as soon as you start looking into  how to actually build these systems you  will find that they are a lot simpler  not only than you think then that you're  used to from traditional architectures  and with that we are out of time  thank you very very much  [Applause]
 

